        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Standing Desk Height for a Walking Pad", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/standing-desk-height-for-walking-pad/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How high should a standing desk be for a walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At about your standing elbow height in shoes plus the height of the pad's belt. For most adults on most pads that is roughly 44 to 54 inches. Measure your elbow height with your arms hanging and elbows at about 90 degrees, add the deck height, and buy a desk that reaches that with an inch or more to spare.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a walking pad raise you?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "By the height of its belt surface, which on most walking pads is roughly 4 to 7 inches. Slim flat pads sit at the low end and incline pads at the top. A powered incline lifts the front further, and your feet rise by roughly half the front rise, so a 5% slope on a 40-inch deck adds about an inch.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a 48-inch standing desk tall enough for a walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For shorter users on a slim pad, often yes. By the estimates in our chart, someone around 5 feet 4 inches on a 4-inch pad needs about 45.5 inches, which a 48-inch desk covers. Someone 5 feet 8 inches on a 6-inch pad needs about 50 inches, which it does not. Measure your own numbers before relying on the chart.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How high should the monitor be on a treadmill desk?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "With the top edge at or slightly below eye level, about an arm's length away. Your eye line sits roughly 0.31 times your height above the keyboard, about 21.5 inches for someone 5 feet 10, whether or not you are on a pad. Many monitors on their own stands sit lower than that and need a sturdy riser.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a keyboard tray help if my desk is too low?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not on its own, because a tray lowers the keyboard below the desktop. Where it helps is the other way round: it lets the desktop and monitor sit higher than your elbows while the keys stay at elbow height, which suits taller users whose screen needs to be well above the keyboard. A sturdy desktop riser is the accessory that adds height.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What elbow angle should I have at a walking desk?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "About 90 degrees, with your upper arms hanging naturally and shoulders relaxed; slightly more open, with the keyboard a touch lower, is fine and many people prefer it. If your shoulders lift or you hunch forward, the desk is at the wrong height. If you have pain, see a physical therapist rather than relying on a rule of thumb.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", url: "https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], jobTitle: "Author and lab tester", worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
