        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people buy a standing desk, slide a walking pad underneath, step on and find
          themselves hunched over a keyboard that is suddenly too low. It is the most common
          treadmill desk mistake, and it is entirely predictable: the pad raises you by its deck
          height, and the desk has to rise by the same amount to keep up. This page turns that
          into numbers you can check before you buy — a formula, a chart of estimated desk
          heights by user height, the matching monitor height, and the reasons so many desks
          stop an inch or two short.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. How High Should a Standing Desk Be for a Walking Pad? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The desk surface should sit at about your standing elbow height in shoes, plus
            the height of the walking pad's belt — so for most adults on most pads, somewhere
            between about 44 and 54 inches.</strong> Taller users and incline pads push the figure
            up; shorter users on slim pads bring it down.
          </p>
          <p>
            Written as a formula: <strong>desk height = standing elbow height (barefoot) + shoe
            sole + deck height</strong>. The elbow height is measured with your upper arms hanging
            and your elbows bent to about 90 degrees. The deck height is how far the belt surface
            sits above the floor, which on most walking pads is roughly 4 to 7 inches. The shoe
            adds about an inch.
          </p>
          <p>
            The arithmetic matters because it decides which desks are usable at all. A desk that
            tops out at around 47 or 48 inches — common on two-stage frames — works for shorter
            users on a slim pad and few others. Three-stage desks that reach around 50 to 52
            inches leave room for most adults. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill desk roundup</a>
            screened its picks on exactly this, and only lists desks that reach about 50 inches
            or more.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page is the working: where each number comes from, a chart for
            heights from 5 feet to 6 feet 6 inches, what the same arithmetic says about your
            monitor, and what to do if your desk comes up short. For setting up and walking at the
            desk once it is the right height — speed, posture, building up your minutes — see our
            <a href="/treadmill-desk-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to using a treadmill desk</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="formula" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Formula: Elbow Height + Shoes + Deck Height</h2>
<p>
            Each of the three terms is simple, but each has a catch.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Standing elbow height</h3>
          <p>
            This is the height of the underside of your forearm above the floor when you stand
            upright with your upper arms hanging naturally and your elbows bent to about 90
            degrees. It is where a keyboard wants to be: at that height your wrists stay roughly
            straight and your shoulders relaxed. Slightly more open than 90 degrees — the keyboard
            a touch lower — is fine and many people prefer it. A keyboard set noticeably higher
            than elbow height pushes the shoulders up, which is the posture behind most "my neck
            hurts after an hour on the treadmill desk" complaints.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the chart below we estimate barefoot standing elbow height as <strong>0.63 times
            overall height</strong>. That is an approximation based on average body proportions,
            the kind of ratio used in ergonomic rules of thumb, and it is only that: people with
            long legs and a short torso, or the reverse, can sit an inch or more either side of
            it. Measuring your own elbow height takes a minute and beats any ratio.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The shoe</h3>
          <p>
            Shoes raise everything — elbows and eyes — by the thickness of the sole under the heel.
            For most athletic shoes that is roughly an inch to an inch and a half, and highly
            cushioned running shoes can be more. The chart uses 1 inch. If you walk in thick
            cushioned shoes, add a little; if you walk in thin-soled shoes, subtract. Do not walk in
            socks or bare feet on a moving belt; our page on
            <a href="/can-you-walk-barefoot-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking barefoot on a treadmill</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deck height</h3>
          <p>
            This is the height of the belt surface you stand on, not necessarily the overall
            height on the box. Walking pads stand roughly 4 to 7 inches tall, with the slimmest
            flat pads at the low end and incline pads at the top — and higher still when the
            incline is raised. A listing's height figure is often the tallest point of the pad,
            which may be the motor cover at the front rather than the belt, so the belt surface can
            sit a little lower. Using the listed height gives you a small safety margin; measuring
            the belt surface with a tape gives you the true figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One more allowance: the keyboard.</strong> The formula gives the height of the
            point where your forearms want to be, and the keys sit on top of the desk, not in it.
            A typical keyboard adds somewhere around half an inch to an inch at the keys, so many
            people end up with the desktop itself about that much below the formula's figure. Treat
            the formula as the height the desk must be able to reach, and fine-tune downwards once
            you are standing on the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="height-chart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Standing Desk Height Chart for a Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            The chart applies the formula to heights from 5 feet to 6 feet 6 inches, using the
            assumptions above: barefoot elbow height of 0.63 times overall height, 1 inch of shoe,
            and three deck heights — 4 inches for a slim flat pad, 6 inches for a typical incline
            pad when level, and 7 inches for a tall pad or an incline pad with the front raised.
            Figures are rounded to the nearest half inch. These are rule-of-thumb estimates; your
            own measurement wins.
          </p>
