              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 ft 8 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">44 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">48 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">50 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">51 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 ft 10 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">45 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">49 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">51 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">52 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 ft 0 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">46.5 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">50.5 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">52.5 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">53.5 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 ft 2 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">47.5 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">51.5 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">53.5 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">54.5 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 ft 4 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">49 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">53 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">55 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">56 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 ft 6 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">50 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">54 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">56 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">57 in</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two patterns are worth noticing. First, each 2 inches of your height adds a little over
            an inch to the desk height you need, because elbow height scales with overall height.
            Second, the pad's deck adds its full height, inch for inch, which is why the jump from
            the "floor" column to any pad column is so large: 4 to 7 inches of extra desk travel
            that a desk bought for standing on the floor was never asked to provide.
          </p>
          <p>
            Remember the keyboard allowance from the previous section. Because the keys sit on top
            of the desktop, many people settle with the desktop half an inch to an inch below the
            figure in the chart. That inch is useful headroom, not a reason to buy a desk that
            only just reaches the chart figure: you want a little travel above your working height
            for adjustments, thicker shoes and the day you try a taller pad.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How to Measure Your Own Numbers</h2>
<p>
            The chart gets you into the right range. Ten minutes with a tape measure gets you the
            real figure, and it is worth doing before you order a desk, a pad or both.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Put on the shoes you will walk in.</strong> The sole is part of the
            arithmetic, so measure in them.</li>
            <li><strong>Stand on a hard floor, relaxed.</strong> Let your upper arms hang by your
            sides, shoulders down, and bend your elbows to about 90 degrees with your forearms
            level and your palms facing down, as if over a keyboard.</li>
            <li><strong>Measure from the floor to the underside of your forearm</strong> just in
            front of the elbow. A second person makes this easier; otherwise, stand beside a wall
            and mark it lightly. This is your elbow height in shoes.</li>
            <li><strong>Measure the pad's belt surface</strong> from the floor to the top of the
            belt in the middle of the deck, where you will stand. If you do not have the pad yet,
            use the overall height from the listing as a conservative figure. For an incline pad,
            note the extra rise at the incline you will actually use at the desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Add the two.</strong> That is the height your forearms need the keyboard to
            be. The desk must be able to reach at least that, ideally with an inch or more to
            spare at the top of its range.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the desk's range with its desktop fitted.</strong> Makers and
            retailers quote height ranges both with and without a desktop, and the difference is
            about the thickness of the top, commonly around an inch.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Once the desk arrives, set it while standing on the pad, not beside it, and adjust in
            small steps over the first few sessions. Most people end up a little lower than their
            first guess once they are walking rather than standing still, because the arms relax
            and the shoulders drop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-too-low" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Why Many Standing Desks Top Out Too Low</h2>
<p>
            Standing desks are designed for people standing on the floor, and for that job a
            maximum of around 47 to 48 inches is generous: from the chart, it covers users up to
            about 6 feet 2 inches. Put a 4 to 7-inch pad under the same desk and its useful range
            drops to roughly the shorter half of adults. Several design choices contribute.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Two-stage legs.</strong> Each leg on a standing desk is a set of telescoping
            tubes. Two-stage legs have two sections and a shorter total travel; three-stage legs
            have three and reach higher from a similar low point. Many lower-priced desks use two
            stages and top out at around 47 or 48 inches, which is why our desk roundup did not
            include a desk under $500 — the cheaper desks it checked stopped in that range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The quoted range is not always what you get.</strong> Some specifications
            give the frame's travel without a desktop; others include a desktop of a stated
            thickness. A frame quoted at 50 inches might reach 51 with a one-inch top fitted, or
            the 50 might already include it. Independent measurements also sometimes differ from
            a maker's figure by half an inch, which our roundup notes for one of its picks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stability falls with height.</strong> Every standing desk moves more near the
            top of its range, because the leg sections overlap less and the joints carry more
            leverage. Walking adds a steady rhythmic push through your forearms. A desk that is
            technically tall enough but has to run at its very top all day will usually wobble
            more than one working comfortably mid-range, which is another reason to want headroom
            above your number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline adds height.</strong> An incline pad stands taller than a flat one, and
            its front rises further when the incline is on. A desk that suits you on the pad when
            it is level can be an inch short when it is tilted.
          </p>
          <p>
            Put together, the practical rule is: <strong>look for a desk whose maximum height,
            with your desktop fitted, is at least an inch above your formula figure</strong>. For a
            user around 5 feet 8 inches on a typical 6-inch incline pad, that means a desk reaching
            about 51 inches; for a 6-foot user on the same pad, about 53 to 54 inches, which is at
            or beyond the top of most standard frames and the point where height extenders or a
            purpose-built treadmill desk come into the conversation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Incline Pads: How Much Extra Height the Slope Adds</h2>
<p>
            A powered incline usually lifts the front of the deck while the rear stays on the
            floor. The extra height under your feet therefore depends on where you stand and how
            steep the slope is. The arithmetic is straightforward: the front of the belt rises by
            roughly the deck length multiplied by the grade, and your feet, which sit somewhere
            near the middle of the deck, rise by about half of that.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>A 40-inch deck at 5%:</strong> the front rises about 2 inches; at the middle
            of the deck you are roughly 1 inch higher.</li>
            <li><strong>A 40-inch deck at 9%:</strong> the front rises about 3.6 inches; you are
            roughly 1.8 inches higher.</li>
            <li><strong>A 42-inch deck at 12%:</strong> the front rises about 5 inches; you are
            roughly 2.5 inches higher.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Those are geometric estimates, and real pads differ in where the pivot sits. As a check
            against a real machine, one hands-on reviewer of the DeerRun Z10 covered in our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline walking pad roundup</a>
            recorded about 6 inches of height level and about 7 inches at full incline, which sits
            comfortably inside these figures.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequence is that incline and typing pull in different directions.
            Most people who use incline at a desk keep it modest during focused work and use the
            steeper settings for walks away from the keyboard. If you do want to type on a slope,
            set the desk height with the incline at the level you will actually use, and be aware
            that a slope also tilts your body very slightly forward relative to the keyboard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="monitor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Monitor Height on a Walking Pad</h2>
<p>
            The usual guidance is to set the monitor so its top edge is at or slightly below eye
            level, about an arm's length away. On a walking pad, your eyes rise by exactly the same
            amount as your elbows — the deck and the shoe lift both — so the monitor has to rise
            with the desk.
          </p>
          <p>
            A neat consequence of the proportions makes this easier to plan. Standing eye height is
            commonly approximated as about 0.94 times overall height, and elbow height as about
            0.63 times. The difference — <strong>roughly 0.31 times your height</strong> — is the
            distance from your keyboard to your eye line, and it does not change whether you are on
            the floor, on a 4-inch pad or on a 7-inch one. So once the desk is at the right height
            for typing, the top of the screen wants to be about that far above the keyboard.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Your height</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Eye line above the keyboard (estimate)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 ft 2 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 19 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 ft 6 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 20.5 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 ft 10 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 21.5 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 ft 2 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 23 in</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6 ft 6 in</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 24 in</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Set the top of the screen at that height or an inch or two below it. Measure your own
            eye line if you can, because this ratio varies between people just as the elbow ratio
            does. Then compare it with your monitor: on many screens on their own stands, the top
            edge ends up lower than this, and the fix is a sturdy riser under the stand or a stand
            with more height adjustment.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two walking-specific points. A screen on its own stand on the desktop usually moves
            less than one on a clamp-on arm, which puts the screen on a lever that nods with every
            step; if you use an arm, choose a rigid one and clamp it near a desk leg. And setting
            the screen a little further back than you would at a sitting desk makes the small
            movements of walking less noticeable. A laptop on its own is the hardest set-up of all:
            if the keyboard is at elbow height, the screen is far too low, so use a separate
            keyboard with the laptop on a stand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="keyboard-tray" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Keyboard Trays and Desktop Risers</h2>
<p>
            Two accessories change the arithmetic, in opposite directions.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A keyboard tray lowers the keyboard below the desktop.</strong> That lets the
            desktop sit higher than your elbows — useful for taller users, whose screen needs to be
            well above the keyboard, and for anyone who wants the monitor raised without a separate
            riser — while the keyboard stays at elbow height. What a tray cannot do is
            raise the keyboard, so it does not rescue a desk that is too low for your hands: on
            such a desk, it makes the keyboard lower still. On a walking desk, a tray also has to
            be rigid. A tray that flexes will bounce with every step, and generic clamp-on trays
            tend to move more than ones designed for walking.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A desktop riser sits on top of the desk</strong> and lifts the keyboard and
            often the monitor with it, typically by several inches. It is the one accessory that
            genuinely adds height to a desk that is too short. The trade-off is stability: a riser
            stacks a second structure on top of the first, and the joint between them is another
            place for movement. If you go this way, choose a heavy, wide riser, keep heavy items
            centred, and test it while walking before you rely on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a desk you are buying new, both are workarounds. A desk whose own range reaches
            your number with room to spare is steadier and simpler than any accessory.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-low-options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. If Your Desk Is Too Low: Your Options</h2>
<p>
            If you already own a desk and the arithmetic says it is short, these are the options,
            roughly from cheapest to most expensive.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Lower the pad, not the standard.</strong> A slim flat pad can be 2 or 3
            inches lower than a tall incline pad. If you have not bought the pad yet, choose the
            slimmest that suits your stride and weight, and leave incline for walks away from
            the desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Check thinner shoes.</strong> A walking shoe with a moderate sole rather
            than a high-stack running shoe can recover half an inch, which sometimes is all you
            need.</li>
            <li><strong>Accept a slightly open elbow angle.</strong> A keyboard an inch below the
            ideal, with elbows opened a little past 90 degrees, is fine for many people. A keyboard
            several inches low is not, because you will hunch.</li>
            <li><strong>Add a sturdy riser</strong>, as above, and test its stability while
            walking.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask the desk maker about extenders.</strong> Some makers sell leg or frame
            extenders that add height; iMovR offers them for its desks.</li>
            <li><strong>Replace the desk</strong> with a three-stage frame whose maximum clears your
            number by an inch or more.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>What not to do:</strong> do not raise the walking pad on blocks, boards or
            books. A pad needs to sit flat and stable on the floor, and a pad propped up is a pad
            that can shift under you. And do not lean your forearms on the desk to make up the
            difference; resting weight on the desk changes your gait and loads your shoulders and
            back. If the desk cannot be made high enough, the desk is the part to change.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our guide to
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmills</a>
            covers choosing a pad with desk height in mind, including how slim the slimmest models
            are.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="comfort-checks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Comfort Checks Once It Is Set Up</h2>
<p>
            Numbers get the desk into the right range; your body tells you whether it is right.
            In the first week, check these while walking at your usual desk speed:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Shoulders down and relaxed</strong>, not lifted towards your ears. Lifted
            shoulders mean the keyboard is too high.</li>
            <li><strong>Wrists roughly straight</strong>, neither bent up to reach the keys nor
            resting hard on the desk edge.</li>
            <li><strong>Elbows near your sides.</strong> If you are reaching forward, bring the
            keyboard closer rather than leaning in; leaning pulls you towards the front of the
            belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Head level</strong>, eyes on the upper third of the screen, without tipping
            your chin down.</li>
            <li><strong>Feet in the middle of the belt</strong>, not drifting to the front roller
            because the desk is pulling you forward.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Change one thing at a time, by half an inch, and give each change a session or two.
            It is normal to want the desk a little different at the end of the day than at the
            start, which is what memory presets on an electric desk are for.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you have discomfort that does not settle with adjustment, or pain, stop and get
            it looked at rather than pushing through. This page can help with the geometry; it
            cannot tell you what suits your body, and a physical therapist or occupational health
            professional can. For speed, how long to walk and building the habit, see our
            treadmill desk guide linked above.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. If You Are Shopping for a Treadmill Desk</h2>
<p>
            Do the sum first, then shop. Write down your formula figure — elbow height in shoes
            plus the pad's belt height — and add an inch. That is the minimum maximum height to
            look for, with the desktop fitted. Then check four more things:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Three-stage legs</strong>, which is usually what it takes to reach
            around 50 inches or more.</li>
            <li><strong>Stability near the top of the range</strong>: four legs, a rigid frame and
            no castors. Independent reviews describing wobble at full height are worth more than
            any marketing claim.</li>
            <li><strong>Clear width between the legs</strong>, not desktop width, against the
            pad's full width including any rail, plus a margin each side. A low crossbar can block
            a pad entirely.</li>
            <li><strong>A 30-inch-deep desktop</strong>, which lets the monitor sit far enough back
            for walking.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our roundup of the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-desks/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmill desks and desks for a walking pad</a>
            applies exactly these checks. It covers two integrated desk-and-treadmill sets and
            three standing desks chosen because they reach about 50 inches or more, states each
            desk's height range where its sources agree, and flags where a maker and a reviewer
            disagree by half an inch — which, as the chart above shows, can be the difference
            between a desk that fits you and one that does not.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How high should a standing desk be for a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At about your standing elbow height in shoes plus the height of the pad's belt. For most adults on most pads that is roughly 44 to 54 inches. Measure your elbow height with your arms hanging and elbows at about 90 degrees, add the deck height, and buy a desk that reaches that with an inch or more to spare.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a walking pad raise you?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                By the height of its belt surface, which on most walking pads is roughly 4 to 7 inches. Slim flat pads sit at the low end and incline pads at the top. A powered incline lifts the front further, and your feet rise by roughly half the front rise, so a 5% slope on a 40-inch deck adds about an inch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 48-inch standing desk tall enough for a walking pad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For shorter users on a slim pad, often yes. By the estimates in our chart, someone around 5 feet 4 inches on a 4-inch pad needs about 45.5 inches, which a 48-inch desk covers. Someone 5 feet 8 inches on a 6-inch pad needs about 50 inches, which it does not. Measure your own numbers before relying on the chart.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How high should the monitor be on a treadmill desk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With the top edge at or slightly below eye level, about an arm's length away. Your eye line sits roughly 0.31 times your height above the keyboard, about 21.5 inches for someone 5 feet 10, whether or not you are on a pad. Many monitors on their own stands sit lower than that and need a sturdy riser.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a keyboard tray help if my desk is too low?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own, because a tray lowers the keyboard below the desktop. Where it helps is the other way round: it lets the desktop and monitor sit higher than your elbows while the keys stay at elbow height, which suits taller users whose screen needs to be well above the keyboard. A sturdy desktop riser is the accessory that adds height.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What elbow angle should I have at a walking desk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 90 degrees, with your upper arms hanging naturally and shoulders relaxed; slightly more open, with the keyboard a touch lower, is fine and many people prefer it. If your shoulders lift or you hunch forward, the desk is at the wrong height. If you have pain, see a physical therapist rather than relying on a rule of thumb.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Setting Desk Height for a Walking Pad</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a good treadmill desk into one that hurts, and all of them are avoidable with a tape measure.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting the desk height on the floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A desk set for you standing beside the pad is 4 to 7 inches too low once you step onto it. Always set the height while standing on the belt, in the shoes you walk in, with your elbows at about 90 degrees, and save it as a preset.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a desk that only just reaches your number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A desk running at the very top of its range all day wobbles more, has no room for thicker shoes or a taller pad, and may be half an inch shorter than its listing. Choose a desk whose maximum, with the desktop fitted, clears your figure by at least an inch.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fixing the keyboard and forgetting the monitor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Raising the desk for your hands raises the monitor too, but rarely enough. A screen left too low makes you tip your head down for hours. Set the top edge at or slightly below eye level, using a sturdy riser if the stand will not go high enough.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Propping the pad up or leaning on the desk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Raising a pad on boards to meet a desk, or resting your forearms on the desk to reach a low keyboard, both trade a geometry problem for a safety or posture one. Keep the pad flat on the floor and change the desk, the pad or the riser instead.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

